1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,586
Our first generator chose each local Jewel from the prompt and its nearby position.

2
00:00:04,586 --> 00:00:10,554
Individual patches often had plausible colors and textures, yet the whole video had no recognizable subject.

3
00:00:10,554 --> 00:00:16,743
It was the visual version of assembling a jigsaw from individually convincing pieces taken from different boxes.

4
00:00:16,743 --> 00:00:21,440
Local correctness does not automatically create one object that survives across time.

5
00:00:22,140 --> 00:00:25,725
We next predicted small blocks of Jewels instead of single ones.

6
00:00:25,725 --> 00:00:32,616
The model became better at predicting which block belonged in each neighborhood, but the rendered result was still texture.

7
00:00:32,616 --> 00:00:37,994
That was useful evidence: the local statistics were improving, while identity was still missing.

8
00:00:37,994 --> 00:00:43,260
Something had to tell distant regions and later frames that they belonged to the same subject.

9
00:00:43,960 --> 00:00:49,706
As a diagnostic test, we assigned one complete training-owned program to the entire spacetime window.

10
00:00:49,706 --> 00:00:56,078
The physical Jewel vocabulary did not change, but ballerina, dog, and welder subjects became recognizable again.

11
00:00:56,078 --> 00:00:58,582
This test was retrieval, not new generation.

12
00:00:58,582 --> 00:01:04,840
Its value was causal: it showed that persistent ownership across blocks and frames was the missing ingredient.

13
00:01:05,540 --> 00:01:08,772
A complete training program could merely replay one example.

14
00:01:08,772 --> 00:01:12,166
To test that, we built a new field from two different programs.

15
00:01:12,166 --> 00:01:16,800
One supplied Jewels near the moving subject tube; the other supplied the surroundings.

16
00:01:16,800 --> 00:01:21,325
When we deliberately swapped in a wrong subject source, the visible subject changed.

17
00:01:21,325 --> 00:01:26,820
The result was not a replay of either complete source, and the tube truly controlled subject identity.

18
00:01:27,520 --> 00:01:29,931
The subject path comes from a saliency map.

19
00:01:29,931 --> 00:01:32,566
Saliency simply means a map of what stands out.

20
00:01:32,566 --> 00:01:40,135
For each time step, we compare the chosen scene with the other scenes and find the region where their learned descriptions differ most.

21
00:01:40,135 --> 00:01:43,163
A small smoothing pass prevents the path from jumping.

22
00:01:43,163 --> 00:01:47,760
This gives us a class-related trajectory without looking at the target test video.

23
00:01:48,460 --> 00:01:51,283
Different fields can pack Jewels at different densities.

24
00:01:51,283 --> 00:01:55,466
A fixed tube width might collect too many from one source and too few from another.

25
00:01:55,466 --> 00:01:57,633
Instead, we sort by distance from the path.

26
00:01:57,633 --> 00:02:03,026
We take the closest thirty-six thousand subject Jewels and the farthest thirty-six thousand setting Jewels.

27
00:02:03,026 --> 00:02:07,260
Ranking guarantees the requested count without duplicates or target-specific tuning.

28
00:02:07,960 --> 00:02:09,677
A pretty sample is not enough.

29
00:02:09,677 --> 00:02:18,719
For every prompt and seed, we generate three fields: one from the correct prompt, one from a deliberately wrong prompt, and one with no prompt meaning at all.

30
00:02:18,719 --> 00:02:27,304
These are causal controls: we change the input that should cause the result, then check whether the generated field changes in the intended direction.

31
00:02:27,304 --> 00:02:30,280
The video itself is regenerated for every condition.
